          "collection_date": "Unknown",
          "description": {
            "comment": "Fasciola hepatica is a species of trematode (Platyhelminthes) that causes liver fluke disease of cattle, sheep and goats throughout most of the temperate regions of the world. Liver fluke disease causes major economic loss to agriculture, including through costs associated with anti-worm medications and development of resistance to anti-fluke drugs. The fluke also affects people within communities in some impoverished regions being heavily afflicted, for example in the Alto Plano region of Bolivia and Peru. The isolate used here was recovered from livers of naturally infected sheep at a commercial abattoir in the state of Oregon, USA. Genomic DNA was recovered from one adult worm.",
